.TH KI 1 "2026-10-07" "Knowledge Islands" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\&
.PP
ki \- Knowledge Islands command-line interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
\&
.SS General
\&
.TP
.B ki [command]
Run a KI command, or print root help when no command is supplied.
.TP
.B ki help [command...]
Print general help or help for a command path.
.SS Global options
\&
.TP
.B --help, -h
Print help for KI or the selected command.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Print the CLI version.
.SS Installation
\&
.TP
.B ki bootstrap [--refresh]
Detect agents, install the canonical harness, and install core user skills.
On refresh, migrate retired local repository paths into machine-local registry
state.
.SS Local management
\&
.TP
.B ki harness search <query>
Search verified installed local harness capabilities.
.TP
.B ki completion <shell>
Print Bash or Zsh completion source for the complete registered CLI grammar.
.TP
.B ki harness outdated
Report installed harnesses only when comparable newer release evidence is
available; it names unavailable evidence rather than claiming freshness.
.TP
.B ki harness missing
Report desired user capabilities that have no installed provider.
.TP
.B ki update [--cli]
Update an installer-managed CLI and refresh installed configured harnesses.
.TP
.B ki cleanup
Report eligible KI-managed stale state.
.TP
.B ki diag [--full]
Show share-safe installation facts; --full includes paths and local identities.
.TP
.B ki doctor
Check user configuration, agents, harnesses, user-skill links, and direct-CWD
legacy KI state and configuration.
.TP
.B ki repair [--apply | --dry-run]
Preview user-skill and configuration repairs; --apply writes them.
.TP
.B ki docs [topic]
Print canonical KI documentation locations.
.TP
.B ki inventory
List verified installed harnesses and capabilities with declared user skills and
local registered repositories.
.TP
.B ki mcp install <owner/repository> [version] [--auth github-cli]
Install and activate one verified MCP source release.
.TP
.B ki mcp update <owner/repository> [version] [--auth github-cli]
Build and activate a newer verified MCP source release.
.TP
.B ki mcp rollback <owner/repository> <version>
Activate one retained complete MCP source version.
.TP
.B ki mcp uninstall <owner/repository>
Remove one validated MCP source installation and its retained versions.
.TP
.B ki mcp list [owner/repository] [--format text|json]
List active and retained MCP source versions.
.TP
.B ki vscode check
Report local VS Code projection drift.
.TP
.B ki vscode sync [--write]
Preview or publish local VS Code projection changes.
.SS User management
\&
.TP
.B ki skill add <skill> [--replace]
Link a harness skill into configured user agent skill spaces.
.TP
.B ki skill remove <skill>
Remove a KI-managed user skill link.
.SS Repository options
\&
.TP
.B ki repo [--repo <path-or-pattern>]... [command]
Use repeated \fB--repo\fR options to select explicit repository roots or
patterns.
.TP
.B ki repo [--agora <name>] [command]
Use the physical KI repositories resolved from one declared named Agora or the
reserved registered-estate selector.
.TP
.B ki repo [--estate] [command]
Select every repository in the registered estate. This is shorthand for
\fBki repo --agora estate\fR.
.SS Repository management
\&
.TP
.B ki repo init [directory] --repository <https-url> --capital <https-url> --title <title> --description <description> --repo-code <code> --runtime <runtime>... --visibility <public|private>
Create the explicit KI identity declaration for one existing Git repository
and register its physical root in the machine-local registry.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] open --target <zed|vscode|delta> [--stores|--no-stores]
Open selected canonical repositories in one local target. Zed and VS Code
include locally registered Knowledge Base external stores by default.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store list [--format <text|json>]
List declared repository stores and their machine-local bindings.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store scan
Warn about selected repositories with undeclared conventional source stores.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store create sources [--write]
Preview or create and bind the managed source-store location.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store bind <sources|legacy> <absolute-path> [--write]
Preview or bind one declared external store.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store unbind <sources|legacy> [--write]
Preview or remove one binding without deleting store content.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] audit [--skill <skill>] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Run registered native repository audits.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] conform [--skill <skill>] [--dry-run] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Record the selected physical KI repository roots locally, then apply
registered native repository repairs.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] diag
Report declared repository skills and compatible projection health without
changing repository or registry state.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] repair [--dry-run]
Record selected physical KI repository roots, then reconcile only their
proven KI-managed projections.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap list [--horizon <horizons>] [--status <status>] [--by <grouping>] [--format <text|json>] [--links <compact|all>]
List governed work items in a framed deterministic lane and lifecycle tree
without changing their lifecycle.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap summary
Show repository-by-lane counts and totals without listing records.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap stats [--stale-after <duration>] [--format <text|json>]
Report timestamp coverage, age, and inactivity for governed work items.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap prune [id] [--no-commit] [--dry-run]
Delete done or cancelled canonical work-item records and commit the deletions.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap promote <id> [horizon]
Move one work item toward the now horizon.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap demote <id> [horizon] [--reason <reason>] [--condition <text>] [--review <date>]
Move one work item toward the hold horizon.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap migrate [--apply]
Preview or apply the mechanical roadmap model migration.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] educate [--skill <skill>]
Explain the registered repository maintenance catalogue.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill add <skill> [--replace]
Link and declare a harness skill in selected repositories.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill remove <skill>
Remove KI-managed repository skill links and declarations.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] upgrade
Refresh uniquely resolved capabilities declared by selected KI repositories.
.SS Batch records
\&
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch prepare --item <id> [--item <id>...] --approved --authority-mode <reviewed-items|outcome> --expires-at <timestamp> --completion-target <awaiting-review|done> [--authority-evidence <text>]
Create one current canonical exact-set batch record from caller-supplied
approval evidence and Ready work items in dependency order.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch validate <record>
Validate a canonical batch record, its protected payload, expiry, run binding,
repository identity, and named work items without writing.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch run <record> [--item <id> --result <awaiting-review|done|parked|stopped> --baseline <commit|—> [--result-commit <commit>] [--exception <text>]]
Start the derived run or append one explicit caller-supplied item result. This
command does not execute agent work or change roadmap lifecycle.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch close <record> --completion-target <awaiting-review|done> --evidence-commit <commit>
Append structural closure evidence only after every named ledger result and
canonical work item matches the approved all-item target. This command does
not accept work.
.SS Knowledge Base search
\&
.PP
Index and search one explicitly registered Knowledge Base
with scoped local citations.
.TP
.B ki kb index [--kb <id>] [--daemon-url <origin>]
Build a fresh private qmd generation for one explicitly assigned registry KB.
.TP
.B ki kb search <query> [--kb <id>] [--mode <query|search|vsearch>] [--limit <count>] [--zone <name>] [--path-prefix <path>]
Return bounded current-source-authenticated JSON results
without silent fallback.
.TP
.B ki kb status [--kb <id>]
Report configured mapping and loopback endpoint reachability;
health does not attest index identity.
.SS Registry management
\&
.TP
.B ki registry [--repo <path-or-pattern>]... [--agora <name>] [--estate] add [--dry-run] [--search-boundary <id>]
Record selected canonical KI repository roots in the machine-local registry
without applying repository repairs.
.TP
.B ki registry list [--format <text|json>]
List registered repository roots as text or emit the path-free
.B ki/registry/v1
contract.
.TP
.B ki registry remove <key> [--dry-run]
Remove one exact machine-local registry entry by key.
.TP
.B ki registry --repo <path> remove [--dry-run]
Remove one exact machine-local registry entry by stored path.
.SS Harness management
\&
.TP
.B ki harness info <harness-id>
Inspect one installed harness and its capabilities.
.TP
.B ki harness list
List installed compatible harnesses.
.TP
.B ki harness install <harness-id>
Install one configured compatible harness.
.TP
.B ki harness reinstall <harness-id>
Replace one inactive installed harness with a verified archive.
.TP
.B ki harness uninstall <harness-id>
Remove one installed non-canonical harness.
.SS Trades
\&
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade routes list [--incomplete] [--format text|json]
List selected local typed trade routes, or aggregate selected declarations
with parent \fB--estate\fR or \fB--agora\fR.
Limit either view to incomplete routes.
Emit versioned machine-readable estate evidence with
.BR "--format json" .
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade routes check [repository] [--direction export|import] [--kind work|knowledge]
Check Capital-granted typed trade routes and their activation state.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade policy show
Show the territory Capital's channels, standing grants, and knowledge
subtypes.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade policy check
Check every territory member against the Capital policy through the
local registry.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade policy compare --baseline <path>
Compare active routes with a saved
.B ki/trade-routes/v1
report.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade standing list [--incomplete]
List Capital-granted standing grants and their activation state.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade standing check [repository] [--direction export|import] [--subtype <subtype>]
Check exact standing knowledge-intake activation.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade standing capture <repository> --subtype <subtype> --source-ref <commit:path#anchor> --capture <path#anchor>
Append one validated receiver-local standing-intake provenance block.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade prepare <repository> --kind work|knowledge --observation unattended|receipt|decision|completion --title <title> --source-ref <reference> --context <text> --submission <text> --constraints <text>
Create one mutable local trade preparation on a declared export route.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade observe <trade-id>
Inspect one sender's committed mutable preparation.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade submit <trade-id>
Freeze one local preparation as an outbound submission.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade abandon <trade-id> --yes
Remove one local mutable preparation.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade receive <trade-id> | --all [--yes]
Receive one committed submission, or preview and confirm an explicit batch.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade list [--direction prepare|import|export] [--status <status>] [--kind work|knowledge]
List visible trade copies in selected repositories, including imports
awaiting receipt.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade show <trade-id>
Show selected visible copies of one trade.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade release <trade-id> | --eligible [--yes]
Release one eligible local outbound submission, or preview and confirm a batch.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade prune <trade-id> | --eligible [--yes]
Prune one eligible local inbound copy, or preview and confirm a batch.
.SS Agora management
\&
.TP
.B ki agora list
List declared named Agoras with their titles, and the registered estate.
.TP
.B ki agora audit [name]
Audit every declared Agora, one named Agora, or the registered estate. Exit 0
when healthy, 1 when findings are reported, and 2 for an invalid or unknown
explicit selector.
.TP
.B ki agora inspect <name> --target <zed|vscode> --workspace <selector>
Compare one explicitly selected local editor workspace with a resolved Agora.
VS Code selectors are absolute physical .code-workspace files; Zed selectors
are decimal workspace IDs. Read-only. Exit 0 for exact, 1 for drift or an
unsupported source, and 2 for invalid selection or resolution.
.TP
.B ki agora show <name> [--verbose]
Show one declared Agora or the registered estate. The default lists member
names;
--verbose also shows canonical repository URLs and local paths.
.TP
.B ki agora roots <name> [--null]
Write absolute physical roots for machine consumption. The default format is
newline-delimited. Use --null or -0 for pathnames that may contain line feeds.
.TP
.B ki agora open <name> --target <zed|vscode|delta>
Open one declared Agora or the registered estate through an explicit local
target. For a named Agora in Zed, add its owner last so the sidebar shows it
first. Delta opens each repository in its running app. Its documented project
flow stores repository and thread data on its servers even with a local model.
.TP
.B ki agora reference set <repository> <checkout> [--dry-run]
Associate one owner-declared repository inclusion with an explicitly selected
absolute local Git checkout whose canonical origin matches.
.TP
.B ki agora reference list
List machine-local external Agora reference associations.
.TP
.B ki agora reference remove <repository> [--dry-run]
Remove only the selected machine-local reference association.
.SS Acquisition
\&
.TP
.B ki acquire images
.RI --adapter\ granola\ [--repo\ <path>]\ --source\ <uuid>\ --directory\ <path>\ --expected\ <count>\ [--dry-run]
.TP
.B ki acquire list
.RI [--repo\ <path>]
.TP
.B ki acquire import
.RI [--adapter\ <name>|--all]\ [--repo\ <path>]\ [--since\ <date>]\ [--until\ <date>]\ [--dry-run]
.TP
.B ki acquire status
.RI [--adapter\ <name>|--all]\ [--repo\ <path>]
.TP
.B ki acquire reconcile
.RI [--adapter\ <name>|--all]\ [--repo\ <path>]
.TP
.B ki acquire reset
.RI [--adapter\ <name>]\ [--repo\ <path>]\ [--source\ <identity>]\ [--component\ <name>]\ [--rebuild]\ [--confirm]
.SS Background agents
\&
.TP
.B ki agent launch <run> <name> <workdir> <prompt-file> [--runtime claude|codex] [--add-dir <dir>...] [--rules none|push|prune|release] [--wait-for <name>...]
Launch one detached agent and write its run packet.
.TP
.B ki agent queue <run> <name> <prompt-file> [--workdir <dir>] [--runtime claude|codex] [--add-dir <dir>...] [--rules none|push|prune|release] [--wait-for <name>...]
Append a ready-made agent to the run queue.
.TP
.B ki agent dispatch <run> --max <count> [--interval <seconds>]
Keep up to the given number of queued agents running, detached.
.TP
.B ki agent wait <run> [--next] [--interval <seconds>]
Block until the next unseen agent, or the whole run, finishes.
.TP
.B ki agent status <run>
Print each agent's latest status and the queue.
.TP
.B ki agent watch <run> [seconds]
Print one status line per interval until the run ends.
.TP
.B ki agent new <run> <name>
Write a prompt skeleton from the run contract.
.TP
.B ki agent decide <run> <text...> [--log <path>]
Append a numbered, dated owner decision.
.SS Development
\&
.TP
.B ki dev local set <harness-id> <local-harness-path>
Validate and remember a checkout for one installed harness without enabling it.
.TP
.B ki dev local on [harness-id]
Switch one remembered installed harness, or all remembered harnesses, to their
local checkouts.
.TP
.B ki dev local off [harness-id]
Restore one remembered harness, or all remembered harnesses, from their
verified archives.
.TP
.B ki dev skill rubric <skill> [--write]
Check or regenerate a skill's rubric catalogue.
.SH DESCRIPTION
\&
.B ki
is the Knowledge Islands command-line interface.
It provides stable, local-first commands for working with Knowledge Islands
material.
This page describes the intended V1 CLI surface.
The concise
.B ki --help
starts with usage, then lists commands alphabetically without task groups.
This manual groups commands by operational scope and purpose. Use
.B ki <command> --help
for focused options,
.B ki docs
for documentation locations, or this page for the complete reference.
.SH COMMAND GROUPS
\&
.PP
KI divides commands into groups by operational scope and purpose, so it is
clear whether a command affects user state, one repository, or local
development state. An asterisk marks a planned interface that is not yet
available in the installed CLI.
.SS General
\&
.PP
Use these commands to invoke KI; use \fB--help\fR for command-specific guidance.
.TP
.B ki [command]
Run a KI command, or print root help when no command is supplied.
.TP
.B ki help [command...]
Print general help or help for a command path, including nested commands.
.SS Global options
\&
.PP
Use these options with
.B ki
or any KI command to print command help or the CLI version.
.TP
.B --help, -h
Print help for KI or the selected command.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Print the CLI version.
.SS Installation
\&
.PP
Use this command to bootstrap the user-managed KI environment.
.TP
.B ki bootstrap
Create the user-managed KI environment configuration from detected supported
agents, install the built-in verified canonical harness, then install the core
user skills —
.BR ki-bootstrap ,
.BR ki-next ,
.BR ki-plan ,
and
.BR ki-recap
— into every configured agent's user skill directory. An existing agent
configuration is reused without replacement. Pass
.B --refresh
to redetect every supported runtime and rebuild the user configuration's
agents, installed harnesses, and KI-managed user skill links.
When the canonical harness is in local development, bootstrap keeps that
binding and installs the core user skills from the local checkout. If the
binding cannot be kept, it prints a warning naming the reason before restoring
the verified archive and re-pointing the configured canonical skills to it.
During a refresh only, KI imports the retired
\fB[repositories].paths\fR list into keyed records in
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/ki/registry.toml\fR and removes that list from user
configuration. Normal repository, trade, and Agora resolution never reads the
retired path list.
.SS Local management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to inspect the local KI installation, user configuration,
and repository state without changing it.
.TP
.B ki harness search <query>
Search only verified installed local harness capabilities. The required
non-empty query matches case-insensitively against a harness identifier,
capability kind, or capability name. Results are sorted by harness identifier,
capability kind, and capability name. The command neither discovers a
repository nor consults a registry or the network. When no capability matches,
it succeeds and reports that no matching installed capabilities exist.
.TP
.B ki completion <shell>
Print Bash or Zsh completion source to standard output. The generated scripts
follow the complete registered command tree, offer valid option names at every
reachable command, complete documented closed values, and delegate repository
selection, capture-directory, and output-directory values to the shell's native
path completion.
They do not invoke KI, consult the network, or invent dynamic identifiers.
.TP
.B ki harness outdated
Report installed harnesses only when comparable newer release evidence is
available; it names unavailable evidence rather than claiming freshness.
.TP
.B ki harness missing
Report desired user capabilities that have no installed provider.
.TP
.B ki update [--cli]
Refresh installed configured harnesses. The executable updates only when a
verified installer receipt proves that it owns the running regular executable.
Use
.B --cli
to require that executable target. Linked development and externally managed
executables, including Homebrew installations, are not self-updated.
.TP
.B ki cleanup
Report only stale artefacts in a persisted, versioned KI-owned format. V1 has
no such format, so this command reports that no eligible managed stale state
exists and changes no files. It does not infer that cache contents,
transaction-looking directories, unconfigured harnesses, links, or unknown
files are stale.
.TP
.B ki diag [--full]
Print share-safe tool/version, proven local/release/unknown installation mode,
executing host platform and architecture, runtime/version, configuration
and registry status,
and counts without changing state. With --full, include local paths, identities,
and detailed configuration diagnostics. It does not inspect repository
declarations or projections.
Local provenance requires a resolved development checkout matching this tool's
source/package layout and a Git checkout or worktree marker. Copied source
without that evidence reports unknown; a source URL alone is insufficient.
.TP
.B ki doctor
Check the user configuration, configured agent skill directories, installed
harnesses, and configured KI user-skill links without changing state. It also
reports direct-CWD legacy \fB.ki-meta/\fR and \fB.ki/\fR directories, without
searching for or operating on a repository. It validates a regular direct-CWD
\fB.ki.toml\fR declaration without resolving its providers. A failing
check produces a non-zero exit status after the complete report. Report the same
context as diag, the read-only scope, verdict, and
pass/warn/fail/skipped counts.
Health does not imply package or release freshness.
.TP
.B ki repair [--apply | --dry-run]
Preview repairs by default; --apply explicitly authorises changes.
Reconcile configured user-skill links from their installed or active local
harness sources. It creates missing links and re-points stale or dangling
symbolic links. It also removes a recognised legacy \fBschema = 1\fR line
from valid user configuration; newly written configuration is unversioned.
It never installs a harness, removes legacy repository state, or repairs
repository projections. It reports
unavailable sources, incompatible agents, and unsafe non-symbolic-link entries
for manual resolution. With \fB--dry-run\fR, it reports proposed links and
schema removal without changing state. Ordinary reads never rewrite
configuration.
.TP
.B ki docs [topic]
Print canonical documentation locations without opening a browser, fetching
content, or discovering a repository. With no topic, it prints every
available location with an explanatory prefix. The supported topics are
.B overview
for
.IR https://knowledgeislands.info/projects/ki/ ,
.B site
for
.IR https://knowledgeislands.info/ ,
.B manual
for
.IR https://github.com/knowledgeislands/tools-ki/blob/main/man/ki.1
and
.B roadmap
for
.IR https://github.com/knowledgeislands/tools-ki/blob/main/ROADMAP.md .
Any other topic is invalid command grammar.
.TP
.B ki inventory
List verified installed harnesses and their capabilities with declared user
skills and local registered repositories. The command does not fetch, discover a
repository, or change state.
.TP
.B ki mcp install <owner/repository> [version] [--auth github-cli]
Install and activate one governed MCP server source release from GitHub. An
explicit version selects only its exact annotated
.B v<SemVer>
tag. With no version, public access resolves the latest stable GitHub Release
without authentication. Private access requires deliberate
.BR --auth github-cli ,
which uses the existing GitHub CLI session for release lookup and clone. KI
never reads, persists, or displays its credential.
.TP
.B ki mcp update <owner/repository> [version] [--auth github-cli]
Require an existing source installation, build the selected release in
same-filesystem staging, and atomically activate it only after provenance,
locked dependency installation, build, and entry-point verification succeed.
Failure leaves the prior active version unchanged. When authentication is not
specified, update retains the active installation's recorded mode.
.TP
.B ki mcp rollback <owner/repository> <version>
Activate one retained complete source version without network access,
dependency installation, or build execution.
.TP
.B ki mcp uninstall <owner/repository>
Validate and remove exactly one source installation, including every retained
version. This command does not alter MCP client bindings.
.TP
.B ki mcp list [owner/repository] [--format text|json]
List active and retained source versions deterministically. Text marks the
active version with
.BR * .
JSON emits the versioned, path-free
.B ki/mcp-sources/v1
projection with repository, tag, full commit, package version, entry point,
installation time, authentication mode, and active state. Source installations
live below
.BR $KI_DATA_HOME/mcp/ ;
complete versions remain available until explicit uninstall.
.TP
.B ki vscode check
Compare chezmoi-managed VS Code workspaces and trusted-folder source state with
the local KI repository registry. Explicit sources bindings join their notes
workspace and trusted-client projection; legacy bindings and unbound directories
are ignored. Preview removal of folder paths that no longer resolve to
directories and deletion of projects with no live folders. Existing projects
outside the registry and intentionally empty workspaces are preserved. Missing
registered checkouts are skipped without changing the registry. Report a source
diff and exit non-zero when drift exists. This command never writes.
.TP
.B ki vscode sync [--write]
Preview the same reconciliation. With
.BR --write ,
publish the reviewed changes to chezmoi source state. This command never runs
.BR "chezmoi apply" .
.SS User management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to inspect installed capabilities and manage the skills
declared in the user-managed KI environment.
.TP
.B ki skill add <skill> [--replace]
Add a registered skill in configured user agent skill spaces.
.TP
.B ki skill remove <skill>
Remove a KI-managed user skill link.
.SS Repository options
\&
.PP
Use this shared option with any
.B ki repo
command to select one or more repositories. It appears after
.B ki repo
and before the command.
.TP
.B --repo <path-or-pattern>
Repeat this option to select literal repository roots or patterns in supplied
order. KI resolves every target physically before an operation begins and
rejects unmatched patterns, invalid roots, and duplicate physical roots.
.TP
.B --agora <name>
Select every member of one declared named Agora, or every registered canonical
repository through the reserved \fBestate\fR selector. This option,
\fB--repo\fR, and \fB--estate\fR are mutually exclusive. Each member must be a physical KI
repository. With neither selector, KI checks a regular direct-CWD
\fB.mgit.toml\fR manifest. An unversioned workspace selects direct members;
an existing schema-one workspace selects its configured group. Both recurse
through child workspaces, follow standard and nested repositories, and skip
bare stores. Existing physical members without \fB.ki.toml\fR are
skipped and named by \fBki repo audit\fR in normal and concise output. Missing
or unsafe checkouts, unsafe or invalid KI declarations, and selections with no
KI repositories fail. A repository manifest falls through to ordinary
single-repository discovery. KI never searches ancestor directories for an
mGit manifest or invokes \fBmgit\fR.
.TP
.B --estate
Select every repository in the registered estate. This is shorthand for
\fB--agora estate\fR and is mutually exclusive with \fB--repo\fR and \fB--agora\fR.
.SS Repository management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to inspect and maintain declared capabilities and manage
their skill links. Read-only operations isolate per-target diagnostics; a later
mutation failure retains earlier successful targets and returns a non-zero
result.
.TP
.B ki repo init [directory] --repository <https-url> --capital <https-url> --title <title> --description <description> --repo-code <code> --runtime <runtime>... --visibility <public|private>
Initialize exactly one existing physical Git worktree root. With no
\fIdirectory\fR, it uses the current directory; an explicit directory must be
that worktree's root and must not be a symbolic link. Every identity value is
required: \fB--title\fR, \fB--description\fR, a stable uppercase
\fB--repo-code\fR, canonical HTTPS GitHub \fB--repository\fR, one or more \fB--runtime\fR values selected from
\fBclaude-code\fR and \fBchatgpt-codex\fR, and \fB--visibility public|private\fR.
It atomically creates the minimal canonical \fBki-repo\fR declaration and
A required \fB--capital\fR names the territory Capital; a Capital names
itself and then also receives \fBterritory_name\fR and \fBterritory_members\fR
in \fB[skills.ki-repo]\fR, listing itself as the first member.
Further members must be kept sorted
ascending in code-point order; an unsorted list is rejected.
registers the physical root in \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/ki/registry.toml\fR. It
never runs
\fBgit init\fR, guesses identity, activates repository skills, creates a
Agora, accepts repository selectors, or overwrites an existing
\fB.ki.toml\fR.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] open --target <zed|vscode|delta> [--stores|--no-stores]
Open every selected canonical repository in the requested local target. By
default, a Knowledge Base opens its notes root followed by every declared,
locally bound external store in declaration order. Each binding must be a direct
directory recorded with the canonical repository; it is never made an estate
member or Agora participant. \fB--no-stores\fR opens canonical notes roots only,
while \fB--stores\fR makes the default explicit. Delta opens repository roots
only; it skips stores by default and rejects \fB--stores\fR.
Delta's documented project flow stores repository and thread data on its
servers even when using a local model.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] audit [--skill <skill>] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Run the native read-only operations registered by each selected repository's
declared capabilities. The host verifies every selected harness inventory and
integrity-covered operation before importing it in process; it never dispatches
repository-vendored runners.
Progress defaults to \fBauto\fR, which follows the output stream's TTY state;
\fBalways\fR also renders deterministic progress in redirected output, and \fBnever\fR
suppresses it.
On a terminal, progress is a compact receipt stream with one mutable activity
row. Its moving bar means work is active; it does not estimate completion from
item count or declared cost. Evidence-ready skills appear once with a full bar,
then collapse to one timed evidence receipt before rubric results begin.
Loading and the operation also finish as timed receipts, with total elapsed time
on the operation receipt. Queued skills are not printed. Use
\fB--progress-style single\fR to suppress temporary per-skill evidence receipts.
Redirected \fB--progress always\fR output defaults to that single-row form.
Progress also names the span in which a skill gathers its evidence, before any
criterion runs, together with whatever stages and steps that skill reports
inside it; a span of unknown extent shows activity and an elapsed clock rather
than a completion estimate.
Per-skill result rows name the assessed repository first and its supplying
harness skill in brackets, and appear only for WARN, FAIL, or FIXED outcomes.
Audit renders FAIL and WARN findings by default;
\fB--reporter-levels\fR accepts a case-insensitive comma-separated selection
from FAIL, WARN, FIXED, INFO, NOT_APPLICABLE, and PASS, or \fBall\fR.
A clean repository summary reports PASS with its skill count.
Diagnostic counters remain for WARN, FAIL, and FIXED outcomes.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] conform [--skill <skill>] [--dry-run] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Stage the same capabilities' safe writes, then apply them and re-audit. All
selected initial audits must pass before the staged conform writes are
published: a blocking finding aborts before publication, leaving every proposed
conform write unapplied. Output labels proposed writes separately from writes
applied after that gate; a dry run validates the complete staged write set and
reports writes it would apply without changing the repository. This boundary
does not include independent local registry updates; it also excludes later
selected repositories, subprocess conforms, and rollback after publication has
started. Publication refuses unsafe, changed, duplicate, or symbolic-link
targets.
Conform uses the same progress controls and reporter-level vocabulary as audit,
names both the target and supplier for every skill, and renders FAIL, WARN, and
FIXED findings by default while retaining every terminal per-skill result.
Its second rubric pass is labelled \fBre-audit\fR. When no write or command was
staged, conform reports that no re-audit is required and stops after the initial
pass.
For a local invocation it records each selected canonical root in the
machine-local registry before evaluating findings. Registration is
an inventory action, not evidence of conformance, so a failing repository
remains available for later repair and bulk maintenance.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap list [--horizon <horizons>] [--status <status>] [--by <grouping>] [--format <text|json>] [--links <compact|all>]
List canonical governed work items from every selected repository without
changing their lifecycle. The framed text output names each selected repository,
then groups items by lane: open adopted records by horizon \fBnow\fR,
\fBnext\fR, \fBsoon\fR, \fBfuture\fR and \fBhold\fR, then \fBtriage\fR
intake, then \fBdone\fR and \fBcancelled\fR records last. Within a lane,
items run from \fBawaiting-review\fR through \fBdraft\fR, with identifiers
breaking ties. The summary line counts the \fBnow\fR lane as a signal with no
cap. Records still in the old shape (a \fBtheme\fR, a \fBwaiting-for\fR,
\fBparked\fR or \fBtriage\fR horizon, \fBwaiting_on_trades\fR or
\fBintake_disposition\fR, or an adopted open record without \fBkind\fR) are
listed in the lane they migrate to, marked \fBlegacy\fR, and counted as
\fBLEGACY\fR; they are tolerated until migration. A \fBcomponent\fR must be
declared in \fB[skills.ki-work-roadmap].components\fR.
Linked items append one compact provider-qualified task key and a count of
additional distinct tickets, preferring implementation links. A legend explains
provider abbreviations. Use \fB--links all\fR to replace the suffix with nested
task entries showing provider, key, and relation, each with its full URL on a
further indented line. Ambiguous keys include authority, scope, and task ID on
separate child lines. These options also apply to \fB--aggregate\fR. JSON
output stays \fBki/roadmap/v1\fR and adds each record's lane, classification,
hold, resolution and legacy shapes. Mappings are read locally without
contacting task systems and do not imply current task status or ownership.
\fB--horizon\fR
and \fB--status\fR filter by lane and lifecycle before rendering; unsupported
values fail with a usage error rather than an empty result.
\fB--horizon\fR accepts a comma-separated list, such as \fBnow,next\fR, or a
repeated flag, and keeps records in any named lane.
\fB--by project\fR
groups text output by each record's \fBproject\fR across the whole selected
set, and implies \fB--aggregate\fR; groups from the same Project in different
repositories merge, and a Project from another territory is qualified with
that territory's name. Each Project group shows its registry
\fBlifecycle\fR, such as \fB[active]\fR.
\fB--by initiative\fR groups
by the Initiative its Project names in the Capital's
\fBStreams/Projects/\fR notes, with Initiatives read from
\fBStreams/Initiatives/\fR, found through the repository's
\fB[skills.ki-repo].capital\fR and the local ki registry; a projectless record
groups under its own \fBinitiative\fR. Records with neither group under
\fBunassigned\fR. An unavailable registry, the retired
\fBStreams/Projects/Initiatives.md\fR index, an unknown slug, or an
\fBinitiative\fR that contradicts the registry is a warning that never changes
the exit status.
\fB--by area\fR
groups by each record's fixed \fBarea\fR under the title that
\fB[skills.ki-work-roadmap.areas]\fR maps to its code; a legacy bare areas list
reads without titles, and JSON output adds \fBareaTitle\fR.
\fB--by\fR does not apply to JSON output.
The roadmap folder's \fBREADME.md\fR index is not read as a work item.
A missing roadmap directory or
malformed or unsafe item is an isolated diagnostic for that repository; other
selected repositories still report, and any roadmap or trade diagnostic makes
the command exit with status 1 after rendering the inventory. The command
never creates, transitions, accepts, prunes, or repairs a work item.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap summary [--by <grouping>]
Show selected repositories as rows against lane columns. Each cell shows
nonzero lifecycle-status counts and a sum, such as d=1 r=2 Σ=3.
The Σ column and row aggregate the same counts per repository and per
lane. A legend expands t (triage), d (draft), r (ready), ip (in-progress),
ar (awaiting-review), x (done), and c (cancelled).
A dash means no items in that cell; a question mark means
unavailable. Repositories without a roadmap are named below the table.
Invalid roadmap evidence
remains diagnostic and makes the command exit with status 1. Item identifiers,
titles, and trade records are omitted.
\fB--by project\fR, \fB--by initiative\fR or \fB--by area\fR replaces the
repository rows with one row per group, aggregated across the selected set as
for \fBroadmap list --by\fR, and appends any grouping warnings.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap stats [--stale-after <duration>] [--format <text|json>]
Report age and inactivity metrics for selected local roadmaps. Every valid work
item requires paired canonical UTC-second creation and update timestamps;
timestamp-free records are diagnostics, not compatibility records. The
\fBNOT_DONE\fR count includes every open item, including triage records, and
excludes done and cancelled ones.
Multiple selected repositories add a selected-set
aggregate. A positive stale duration uses seconds, minutes, hours, or days,
such as
.BR 7d .
Use
.B --format json
for a versioned machine-readable report.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap prune [id] [--no-commit] [--dry-run]
Without an ID, delete every canonical work-item record whose lifecycle status
is
.B done
or
.B cancelled
from the selected repositories. It leaves every open lifecycle status,
non-canonical file, and peer repository untouched. If no selected repository
contains a terminal record, it reports that no record was pruned. With an ID,
require exactly one selected repository and delete only that named done or
cancelled record. By default, each repository with pruned records gets one
commit that contains exactly those deletions, with the subject
.B chore(roadmap): prune <N> done work record(s)
whichever terminal state the records reached, and one
.B - <ID>
body line per record; commit hooks run. Before deleting anything, the command
refuses when a selected repository is not a Git work tree, has staged changes,
or holds a selected record that is untracked or modified, so each record's
terminal state is committed before the prune commit. A failed commit
restores that repository's records, and a commit that a hook widened with other
paths is reported as an error.
.B --no-commit
only deletes the records and skips the Git checks.
.B --dry-run
makes the same selection and checks, then reports the records and planned
commits without deleting or committing.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap promote <id> [horizon]
Move exactly one selected repository's named work item one horizon toward
.B now
along now, next, soon, future and hold.
With an optional destination horizon, move directly only toward
.BR now ;
the current horizon, a hold-facing destination, and the now endpoint are
errors. Leaving
.B hold
re-decides the horizon, so it requires a named destination and removes the
record's hold mapping. Triage records are adopted through the planning
workflow, done and cancelled records have no horizon, and records still at a
legacy horizon must be migrated first. The destination must be allowed for the
record's status: ready records stay within now, next and hold, and in-progress
and awaiting-review records within now and hold. The command changes only the
item's horizon frontmatter and its update timestamp.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap demote <id> [horizon] [--reason <reason>] [--condition <text>] [--review <date>]
Move exactly one selected repository's named work item one horizon toward
.BR hold .
With an optional destination horizon, move directly only toward
.BR hold ;
the current horizon, a now-facing destination, and the hold endpoint are
errors. A move into
.B hold
requires
.B --reason
.RB ( waiting-for
or
.BR parked )
and a
.B --condition
naming the release condition, with an optional ISO
.B --review
date; these options are rejected for any other destination. The same lifecycle
and status rules as
.B promote
apply.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] roadmap migrate [--apply]
Preview the mechanical roadmap model migration for every selected repository,
printing each changed record's removed and added frontmatter lines and the
review notes it needs. The pass moves a
.B triage
horizon on a draft to
.BR "status: triage" ;
a
.B waiting-for
or
.B parked
horizon on an open record to
.B horizon: hold
with a hold reason and a condition lifted from the record prose, or a
.B REVIEW
placeholder when none is found;
.B waiting_on_trades
to
.BR hold.trades ;
and a done Triage intake disposition to
.B status: cancelled
with its
.B resolution
and any
.BR resolution_target ,
noting that a Cancelled section is still needed. It advances
.BR updated_at ,
never touches
.BR theme ,
.BR kind ,
.BR project ,
or
.BR purpose ,
and a migrated record needs no further mechanical change, so a second run
reports nothing.
.B --apply
writes the records in exactly one selected repository whose roadmap has no
unreadable record, and never stages or commits. Unreadable records are
diagnostics that make the command exit with status 1.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store list [--format <text|json>]
List every store role declared by selected Knowledge Bases. The \fBnotes\fR role
is the repository root; external roles show their explicit machine-local path
or unbound state. JSON emits the versioned
\fBki/repository-stores/v1\fR contract.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store scan
Inspect selected repositories for direct conventional OneDrive
\fBsources-<repository-basename>\fR directories without a matching declared
\fBsources\fR store role. Warnings identify the repository kind and an explicit
decision to declare and bind, migrate to a Knowledge Base, or retire the
directory; warnings exit successfully. Unsafe paths or unreadable repository
declarations remain diagnostics and cause a non-zero exit. Repository
selection follows normal \fBki repo\fR semantics, including \fB--estate\fR
for every registered repository. The command never creates, binds, moves, or
removes a directory.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store create sources [--write]
Preview the conventional opt-in OneDrive
\fBsources-<repository-basename>\fR directory and registry binding. With
\fB--write\fR, create and bind it. Automatic creation is unavailable for
\fBlegacy\fR.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store bind <sources|legacy> <absolute-path> [--write]
Preview an explicit binding for one declared external store. The path must be
an existing absolute direct directory. With \fB--write\fR, publish the binding
to the machine-local registry.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] store unbind <sources|legacy> [--write]
Preview removal of one declared external-store binding. With \fB--write\fR,
publish the registry change without deleting or modifying store content.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] educate [--skill <skill>]
Explain each selected repository's declared capabilities and their recommended
maintenance actions without changing repository state.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] diag
Report each selected repository's declared skills and compatible runtime
projection health without changing repository or registry state. It returns a
non-zero status when a declaration or projection is unrepairable.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] repair [--dry-run]
Record each selected physical repository root in the local user register, then
reconcile only missing, dangling, or stale KI-managed compatible projections.
It never creates a declaration, adds an undeclared skill, overwrites foreign
content, guesses a provider or runtime, or follows a symbolic declaration.
The command writes by default. \fB--dry-run\fR reports the same actions
without creating state directories or changing disk state. Store mutations
and VS Code sync instead preview by default and require \fB--write\fR.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill add <skill> [--replace]
Add a registered skill in each selected repository.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill remove <skill>
Remove a KI-managed repository skill link and declaration from each selected
repository.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] upgrade
Refresh uniquely resolved harness providers declared by each selected KI
repository. It changes neither user nor repository skill activation.
.SS Batch records
\&
.PP
Use these commands to maintain one already-approved exact set of Ready local
work records. They validate and record caller-supplied authority evidence; they
do not select work, execute an agent, accept delivery, prune work, push, or
release.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch prepare --item <id> [--item <id>...] --approved --authority-mode <reviewed-items|outcome> --expires-at <timestamp> --completion-target <awaiting-review|done> [--authority-evidence <text>]
Create one current canonical exact-set batch authority record from explicit
approval evidence and Ready work items in dependency order.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch validate <record>
Validate an open batch against live canonical work records and its expiry. A
closed batch validates its named outcomes against the immutable close-evidence
commit, so later legitimate pruning does not erase the delivery proof.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch run <record> [--item <id> --result <awaiting-review|done|parked|stopped> --baseline <commit|—> [--result-commit <commit>] [--exception <text>]]
Start the derived run or append one explicit caller-supplied item result without
executing work or changing roadmap lifecycle.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] batch close <record> --completion-target <awaiting-review|done> --evidence-commit <commit>
Append structural closure evidence only after every named live item and the
exact evidence commit contain the approved all-item target in valid dependency
order.
.SS Knowledge Base search
\&
.PP
Index and search one explicitly registered Knowledge Base
with scoped local citations.
.TP
.B ki kb index [--kb <id>] [--daemon-url <origin>]
Build a fresh private qmd generation for one explicitly assigned registry KB.
.TP
.B ki kb search <query> [--kb <id>] [--mode <query|search|vsearch>] [--limit <count>] [--zone <name>] [--path-prefix <path>]
Return bounded current-source-authenticated JSON results
without silent fallback.
.TP
.B ki kb status [--kb <id>]
Report configured mapping and loopback endpoint reachability;
health does not attest index identity.
.SS Registry management
\&
.PP
Use these root-level commands to record and list canonical KI repository roots
in \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/ki/registry.toml\fR. Registry membership is an
inventory, not a
repository-operation or conformance verdict. The repository key names each
record; each record holds a canonical HTTPS GitHub repository identity and
physical checkout path.
.TP
.B ki registry [--repo <path-or-pattern>]... [--agora <name>] [--estate] add [--dry-run] [--search-boundary <id>]
Record selected canonical KI repository roots in
\fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/ki/registry.toml\fR without applying repository repairs.
This explicit inventory action validates the selected root's declared canonical
identity and preserves existing entries. It never
searches outside the supplied target selection.
The command writes by default; \fB--dry-run\fR reports proposed registration
without creating the state directory or changing disk state.
.TP
.B ki registry list [--format <text|json>]
List physical KI repository roots explicitly registered in the machine-local
registry, one absolute path per line. The command neither discovers
repositories nor changes state. With
.BR "--format json" ,
emit the versioned path-free
.B ki/registry/v1
contract with canonical identity and declared repository metadata, including
.B repoType
as
.B project
or
.B kb
for available repositories and
.B null
for unavailable ones. An unavailable declaration remains represented without
a local path and makes the command exit non-zero.
.TP
.B ki registry remove <key> [--dry-run]
Remove exactly one complete registry entry by its local key. The command reports
the removed key, repository identity, and stored path, and leaves every other
entry unchanged.
.TP
.B ki registry --repo <path> remove [--dry-run]
Remove exactly one complete registry entry by its stored path, including when
the checkout no longer exists. Removal rejects bulk selectors and publishes the
validated replacement atomically. Use
.B --dry-run
to inspect the write without changing registry state.
.SS Harness management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to install, inspect, and remove compatible harness payloads
in the user-managed KI environment.
.PP
A configured private GitHub archive may declare
.B auth = "github-cli"
only with its matching commit-pinned
.B https://codeload.github.com/<owner>/<repository>/tar.gz/<revision>
URL. KI obtains the credential through
.B gh auth token
and sends it only on that no-redirect archive request; it neither stores nor
prints the token. Authenticate the GitHub CLI first with
.BR gh\ auth\ login .
.TP
.B ki harness info <harness-id>
Inspect one user-installed compatible harness and its capability
inventory without changing user state.
.TP
.B ki harness list
List compatible harnesses already installed.
.TP
.B ki harness install \fIharness-id\fR
Install a harness from which capabilities, such as skills and subagents, can
be added.
.TP
.B ki harness reinstall <harness-id>
Replace one inactive installed Harness with a verified archive. The command
refuses a Harness that supplies active user skills. A development-linked
Harness must first be restored with
.B ki dev local off <harness-id>.
.TP
.B ki harness uninstall <harness-id>
Remove one non-canonical harness with only recognised payload directories. The
canonical harness cannot be removed, and a harness that supplies active user
skills cannot be removed. A successful removal deletes its identifier from
.BR [harnesses].ids .
.SS Trades
\&
.PP
Use these commands with repositories that declare the portable
\fBki-repo-trades\fR capability and are present in the local registered repository
estate. Routes and standing grants come only from the territory Capital's
\fB[skills.ki-trades.territory]\fR policy, resolved through each repository's
declared \fB[skills.ki-repo].capital\fR; no command edits that policy, and a
member declaration carries no route table. Parent \fB--repo\fR, \fB--agora\fR,
and \fB--estate\fR selectors scope reads; mutations require exactly one selected
registered repository. Every mutation writes only the selected repository, a
granted route gives visibility rather than acceptance or retention, and no
command changes a peer roadmap or knowledge.
.PP
A repository that declares \fBki-trades\fR but whose Capital does not resolve
is never dropped silently. Aggregate \fBroutes list\fR and \fBtrade list\fR
views, and \fBpolicy compare\fR, print
\fBskipped: <identity> (<reason>)\fR for it and add \fBSKIPPED=\fR\fIn\fR to
the summary; under \fB--format json\fR, and for \fBtrade show\fR, the line is
written to standard error so standard output is unchanged. Selected alone,
such a repository fails closed with the reason.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade routes list [--incomplete] [--format text|json]
List selected local typed routes with their registered-estate state. With a
parent aggregate selection such as \fBki repo --estate trade routes list\fR,
list valid selected repository declarations as lexical repository pairs.
The left and right endpoint cells span the two directional rows, which show
what each repository sends with a right or left arrow; a missing direction is
shown as an em dash. The table adapts to the live terminal width, preserving
the same pairs in a stacked narrow view. With
.BR --incomplete ,
show only routes that await reciprocity or have ambiguous peers. With
.BR "--format json" ,
emit the versioned \fBki/trade-routes/v1\fR contract. JSON requires
an aggregate parent repository selection. It contains canonical identities,
route evidence, peer resolution, and declared
map bonuses without local registry paths, declaration paths, or presentation
layout values. Text remains the default renderer. Interactive visualisation is
owned by Knowledge Islands Observatory rather than this command.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade routes check [repository] [--direction export|import] [--kind work|knowledge]
Check the routes the territory Capital policy grants this repository. A route
is active when its peer is registered once, declares \fBki-trades\fR, and
resolves the same Capital; otherwise an export awaits the receiver and an
import awaits the sender.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade policy show
Show the trade policy of the selected repository's territory Capital: its
channels, standing grants, and knowledge subtype vocabulary. The policy is
resolved through the repository's declared \fB[skills.ki-repo].capital\fR; an
unregistered Capital fails with
\fBterritory policy lives in <url>, not available here\fR.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade policy check
Sweep the selected repository's territory through the local registry. Each
listed member is reported conforming, warning (declares \fBki-trades\fR but
no channel names it), failing (registered twice, invalid, declaring another
Capital, or named by a channel without declaring \fBki-trades\fR), or
unverifiable (not checked out here). A registered repository that claims the
Capital without being listed also fails. Any failure exits 1 after rendering.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade policy compare --baseline <path>
Compare the active directed routes of a saved
.B ki/trade-routes/v1
report with the routes the current registry and Capital policies make active,
listing lost and newly granted routes. The current side always sweeps the whole
local registry, whatever the parent repository selection. A baseline route
lacking string source and peer repositories, an \fBexport\fR or \fBimport\fR
direction, or a known trade kind is rejected as malformed. A lost route exits 1
after rendering.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade standing list [--incomplete]
List the standing grants the territory Capital policy gives this repository.
A grant is active exactly when its ordinary knowledge route is active. With
.BR --incomplete ,
show only grants that are not active.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade standing check [repository] [--direction export|import] [--subtype <subtype>]
Check all Capital-granted standing grants or select one repository,
direction, and subtype.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade standing capture <repository> --subtype <subtype> --source-ref <commit:path#anchor> --capture <path#anchor>
From the receiver repository, append one marked
.B ki-trades/standing-intake/v1
TOML provenance block to an existing local Markdown file. The command requires
an active exact standing import, a full source commit, and a source path that
resolves in the locally registered source checkout. It never writes to the
source repository. Standing intake grants no roadmap, publication,
implementation, acceptance, completion, Agora, or peer-write authority; use an
itemized knowledge trade when the exact standing contract does not fit.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade prepare <repository> --kind work|knowledge --observation unattended|receipt|decision|completion --title <title> --source-ref <reference> --context <text> --submission <text> --constraints <text>
Create one mutable preparation in the current repository when it declares the
matching export route. The mandatory observation policy selects whether receipt,
a terminal decision, or completion of adopted work is required before release;
\fBunattended\fR requires no receiver follow-up beyond an observable receipt.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade observe <trade-id>
Read one sender's committed mutable preparation without receiving or acting on
it. Compare it with the commit last observed by this receiver, or show the full
current contents when no usable earlier Git evidence exists.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade submit <trade-id>
Freeze one local preparation and move it to the outbound submitted path.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade abandon <trade-id> --yes
Remove one unsubmitted mutable preparation after explicit confirmation.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade receive <trade-id> | --all [--yes]
Import one committed outbound submission into the current repository and record
its source commit. The \fB--all\fR form previews every receivable submission and
changes nothing unless \fB--yes\fR is also supplied.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade list [--direction prepare|import|export] [--status <status>] [--kind work|knowledge]
Read visible preparations, imports, and exports in selected repositories,
including each selected receiver's imports awaiting receipt. The parent
\fB--estate\fR selector combines all registered repository views. Each record
reports its observation policy, delivery and decision state, and cleanup
eligibility.
An unsupported \fB--status\fR decision value fails with a usage error.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade show <trade-id>
Show selected visible copies of a trade; use parent \fB--estate\fR to
inspect all registered copies.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade release <trade-id> | --eligible [--yes]
Remove one current-repository outbound submission only after its observation
policy is satisfied. The \fB--eligible\fR form previews all eligible submissions
and changes nothing unless \fB--yes\fR is also supplied.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] trade prune <trade-id> | --eligible [--yes]
Remove one eligible current-repository inbound copy only after sender release is
observable. The \fB--eligible\fR form previews all eligible copies and changes
nothing unless \fB--yes\fR is also supplied.
.SS Agora management
\&
.PP
Named Agoras are declared by registered owner repositories under
\fB[skills.ki-agora.<id>]\fR with a required single-line \fBtitle\fR that is
shown to people; the identifier remains the only selector and machine key. The
owner lists direct members; members need no Agora declaration. Optional
\fBincludes\fR entries add another Agora's owner and direct members or one
repository to the working set without membership.
KI deduplicates and orders projected roots alphabetically by registry key.
\fBestate\fR is the reserved selector for all locally registered canonical KI
repositories.
.TP
.B ki agora list
List declared named Agoras with their titles, and the registered estate. The
declaring owner is not counted among direct members. The estate counts
registered repositories; \fBREGISTERED_REPOSITORIES\fR is that deduplicated
total. Resolved and unresolved inclusions have separate counts.
.TP
.B ki agora audit [name]
Audit every declared Agora, one named Agora, or the registered estate without
changing repository or registry state. Exit 0 when healthy, 1 when findings
are reported, and 2 for an invalid or unknown explicit selector.
.TP
.B ki agora inspect <name> --target <zed|vscode> --workspace <selector>
Compare one explicitly selected local editor workspace with a resolved Agora.
VS Code selectors are absolute physical .code-workspace files; Zed selectors
are decimal workspace IDs. Read-only. Exit 0 for exact, 1 for drift or an
unsupported source, and 2 for invalid selection or resolution.
.TP
.B ki agora show <name> [--verbose]
Show one declared Agora with its title, owner, direct members, resolved
inclusions, and unresolved-inclusion diagnostics in separate sections. The
estate lists registered repositories without a home. \fB--verbose\fR also
shows canonical repository URLs and local paths, including the home's path.
.TP
.B ki agora roots <name> [--null]
Write absolute physical roots for machine consumption. The default format is
newline-delimited. Use --null or -0 for pathnames that may contain line feeds.
.TP
.B ki agora open <name> --target <zed|vscode|delta>
Open one declared Agora or the registered estate through an explicit local
target. For a named Agora in Zed, add its owner last so the sidebar shows it
first; the estate and VS Code keep canonical projection order. Delta opens each
repository in its running app. Its documented project flow stores repository
and thread data on its servers even with a local model.
.TP
.B ki agora reference set <repository> <checkout> [--dry-run]
Associate one owner-declared repository inclusion with an explicitly selected
absolute local Git checkout whose canonical origin matches. The operation
never registers, clones, or mutates the included repository.
.TP
.B ki agora reference list
List machine-local external Agora reference associations.
.TP
.B ki agora reference remove <repository> [--dry-run]
Remove only the selected machine-local reference association.
.SS Acquisition
\&
.PP
Acquisition is action-first and adapter-driven. Installed Harness skills
publish verified machine-readable adapter declarations; repository
configuration enables them.
.TP
.B ki acquire images
.RI --adapter\ granola\ [--repo\ <path>]\ --source\ <uuid>\ --directory\ <path>\ --expected\ <count>\ [--dry-run]
Verify UUID-named image exports from the Granola desktop app against an
already acquired meeting. Preserve their exact bytes and a checksum manifest
beside its Markdown in the receiving Knowledge Base. The expected count is
observed in the desktop Images stack, not supplied by Granola MCP.
.TP
.B ki acquire list
.RI [--repo\ <path>]
List enabled, available, and invalid acquisition adapters with owning
skill, actions, configuration, executable status, and activation guidance.
.TP
.B ki acquire import
.RI [--adapter\ <name>|--all]\ [--repo\ <path>]\ [--since\ <date>]\ [--until\ <date>]\ [--dry-run]
Run one selected adapter, every applicable enabled adapter, or infer the
sole enabled adapter. Adapter-specific invocation properties require
explicit
.BR --adapter .
ChatGPT accepts
.B --capture
and
.BR --output .
Granola accepts
.BR --refresh-transcripts .
.TP
.B ki acquire status
.RI [--adapter\ <name>|--all]\ [--repo\ <path>]
Report local authoritative checkpoints, in-progress journals, transcript
states, and post-acquisition dispositions without contacting the provider.
.TP
.B ki acquire reconcile
.RI [--adapter\ <name>|--all]\ [--repo\ <path>]
Verify local checkpoint and disposed-document evidence without mutating
provider or repository state.
.TP
.B ki acquire reset
.RI [--adapter\ <name>]\ [--repo\ <path>]\ [--source\ <identity>]\ [--component\ <name>]\ [--rebuild]\ [--confirm]
Preview a local adapter, source, component, or complete-rebuild reset.
Mutation requires
.B --confirm
and never authorizes provider mutation.
.PP
The Granola adapter uses only allowlisted read-only MCP operations. It
enumerates complete global and folder identity evidence, revalidates
mutable detail, reuses verified transcripts by default, records separate
detail and transcript hashes, journals interrupted work atomically, and
advances the authoritative checkpoint only after the selected generation
verifies. It stages meeting Markdown beneath
.IR +/_ACQUIRE/granola/ .
Conflicting receiver mappings fail closed; acquisition never tags, edits,
archives, deletes, or moves Granola data.
.SS Background agents
\&
.PP
Launch and coordinate detached background agents under the
.B ki-delegation
background-run contract. Each run lives in
.IR $KI_STATE_HOME/agents/<run>/ ,
with a prompt, one-line status, pid, log, and report per agent. An agent
writes
.B DONE
as the last line of its status when it finishes. Runtime adapters start
Claude Code or Codex in their own session with standard input from
.IR /dev/null ,
so an interrupt of the launching session cannot stop them.
.TP
.B ki agent launch <run> <name> <workdir> <prompt-file> [--runtime claude|codex] [--add-dir <dir>...] [--rules none|push|prune|release] [--wait-for <name>...]
Launch one detached agent. The prompt gains a wait gate for each
.B --wait-for
agent, the authority footer the pinned Harness's
.B ki-delegation
skill ships for the
.B --rules
tier (default
.BR none ),
and the progress protocol. The status time zone follows
.BR TZ .
.TP
.B ki agent queue <run> <name> <prompt-file> [--workdir <dir>] [--runtime claude|codex] [--add-dir <dir>...] [--rules none|push|prune|release] [--wait-for <name>...]
Append a ready-made agent to the run queue, copying its prompt. Queue order
is priority order.
.TP
.B ki agent dispatch <run> --max <count> [--interval <seconds>]
Start a detached dispatcher that keeps up to
.I count
agents running, launches the next queued agent as soon as one finishes,
logs each launch to
.IR dispatch.log ,
and exits when the queue is empty and nothing is running.
.TP
.B ki agent wait <run> [--next] [--interval <seconds>]
With
.BR --next ,
block until the next unseen agent finishes, print
.I "<name> finished: <last status>"
or that it exited without DONE, and mark it seen. A coordinator runs it in
the background, reports each finish, and re-arms it. Without
.BR --next ,
wait for the whole run. Either form prints
.B ALL FINISHED
when nothing is left to wait for.
.TP
.B ki agent status <run>
Print each agent's latest status, flag any agent whose process exited
without DONE, and list the queue.
.TP
.B ki agent watch <run> [seconds]
Print one line per interval (default 120 seconds) until no agent is
running, ending with
.BR "ALL FINISHED" .
.TP
.B ki agent new <run> <name>
Write a prompt skeleton from the
.B ki-delegation
run contract to
.IR <name>.draft.md .
.TP
.B ki agent decide <run> <text...> [--log <path>]
Append a numbered, dated owner decision to the run's
.I decisions.md
or the named log, for prompts to cite as their authority.
.SS Development
\&
.PP
Use these commands to switch Harnesses already present in the installed KI
estate between their verified archives and independent local development
checkouts.
.TP
.B ki dev local set <harness-id> <local-harness-path>
Validate the identity against the installed estate and remember it with the
local checkout without changing the active payload or managed user-skill links.
When the selected harness is the canonical harness, the checkout must contain
the core bootstrap skill at
.IR skills/keystone/ki-bootstrap/SKILL.md
and physical
.IR skills ,
.IR subagents ,
and
.IR hooks
payload directories.
.TP
.B ki dev local on [harness-id]
Enable selected local checkouts for development. KI replaces each selected
Harness's complete active root with its checkout, so its metadata and payloads
always have one source, records each identity and path below
.BR [locals] ,
and reconciles its managed user-skill links to the local source on every run.
An explicit Harness ID selects one source; omitting it selects all remembered
sources.
.TP
.B ki dev local off [harness-id]
Remove each selected recognised local Harness root, reinstall its verified
archive, retain its entry below
.BR [locals] ,
and reconcile its managed user-skill links to the restored archive. An explicit
Harness ID selects one source; omitting it selects all remembered sources.
.TP
.B ki dev skill rubric <skill> [--write]
Check or regenerate a skill's rubric catalogue.
.SH PATHS
\&
.PP
Each KI path uses the first non-empty value:
.nf
$KI_DATA_HOME   | $XDG_DATA_HOME/ki   | ~/.local/share/ki
$KI_CONFIG_HOME | $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ki | ~/.config/ki
$KI_CACHE_HOME  | $XDG_CACHE_HOME/ki  | ~/.cache/ki
$KI_STATE_HOME  | $XDG_STATE_HOME/ki  | ~/.local/state/ki
.fi
Run
.B ki diag
to inspect the effective values.
.SH FILES
\&
.TP
.I .ki.toml
Required repository declaration at a KI repository root.
\fB[repo]\fR names the harnesses that provide the repository's skills, and each
governing skill is declared by its bare name under \fB[skills]\fR, carrying its
own configuration. A provider must appear in the Harness list before its skill
can be added; Harness-qualified skill keys are invalid. Installed Harnesses
declare distinct capability prefixes through \fB[skills.ki-repo-harness]\fR in
their own root configuration.
Repository identity and metadata live in \fB[skills.ki-repo]\fR, and trade
routes in \fB[skills.ki-trades.routes]\fR, keyed once per partner.
.SH DEVELOPMENT INSTALLATIONS
\&
.PP
After the first verified release, the Homebrew tap will install the same regular
release executable.
For a direct verified install, download
.B install.sh
from an exact KI release tag and run it with that tag, for example
.BR "bash install.sh v1.2.3" .
The installer verifies the Ed25519-signed checksum manifest before it downloads
and installs the target archive. It supports Apple Silicon and Intel macOS, and
x86_64 glibc Linux.
For development against this checkout, run
.B ./install.sh --link
to place a symbolic link in
.B KI_CLI_INSTALL_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.local/bin ).
The linked command executes the current TypeScript source through Bun without
changing its release version, and links the manual into
.B KI_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
(default: the corresponding
.IR share/man/man1
directory).
Use a dedicated development command directory or invoke
.B ./bin/ki
directly when comparing it with Homebrew's installed executable. The linked
source mode requires Bun; a verified release executable does not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\&
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An operational check or requested operation failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command grammar was invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
\&
.PP
The project README and the Knowledge Islands public tooling guide.
